+// covers the whole access (mutation 1 moves the push16 check to the second byte). A refused push writes
+// nothing and leaves S; a refused pop reads nothing, leaves S, and returns 0. The rest of the instruction
+// still runs (the caller uses the 0), so the CPU state at the stop is NOT the pre-instruction state.
